+<section id="result-inherited-input-and-scope" class="level1">
+<h1>1. Result, inherited input, and scope</h1>
+<p>The remaining quantity in the current endpoint criterion is a ratio of four canonical arithmetic quotient volumes. This note constructs its underlying restriction operator, proves composition and action identities for that operator, and gives two further ways to calculate and bound the same volume. No new arithmetic metric is selected.</p>
+<p>The principal map is</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-1"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ T_{i,j}:(E,G_j)\longrightarrow(E,G_i),\qquad
+ T_{i,j}=K_iG_j,\quad K_n=G_n^{-1},\quad i\le j.                 \tag{1}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-1">(1)</a></span></p>
+<p>It is the <strong>metric adjoint of the original arithmetic identity transport</strong> from degree <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> to degree <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. It is also the map read by restricting the actual degree-<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> canonical polynomial representative to degrees at most <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-2"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>pr</mo><mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ R_iT_{i,j}=\operatorname{pr}_{\le i}R_j.                       \tag{2}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-2">(2)</a></span></p>
+<p>The source coordinate inclusions in (2) are made explicit below. The return maps compose in the reverse direction,</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-3"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mo>det</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>/</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>det</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ T_{i,j}T_{j,l}=T_{i,l},\qquad
+ \det T_{i,j}=V_j/V_i,\quad V_n=\det G_n.                        \tag{3}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-3">(3)</a></span></p>
+<p>Their eigenvalues <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i,j,a}</annotation></semantics></math> are in <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and have an exact source meaning: they are the squared singular values of the original identity transport equipped with its two actual quotient metrics, and also of the polynomial restriction on the canonical image. Hence</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-4"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mo>log</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>/</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>−</mi><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>dim</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></munderover><mo>log</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{\log(V_i/V_j)=-\sum_{a=1}^{\dim E}\log g_{i,j,a}.}       \tag{4}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-4">(4)</a></span></p>
+<p>No arithmetic vector is killed by <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> at a finite admitted degree. Its small eigenvalues quantify the cost of recovering the original arithmetic coordinates from a lower-degree portion of their representatives.</p>
+<p>The new finite upper bounds express (4) through positive traces of powers of <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I-T_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>, with an explicitly certified lower bound on <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i,j,a}</annotation></semantics></math>. A Cauchy–Binet expansion gives another exact representation, by nonnegative full-jet minor weights. The expansion retains the literal mass of the arithmetic source and does not assign its total weight the value one.</p>
+<p>The preceding analytic continuation proved, for a fixed full packet <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\ge k\ge3</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-5"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mi>n</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \left(\frac{\omega_{h,k,2n}}{\omega_{h,k,n}}\right)^{1/(2n)}
+ \le C_h n.                                                     \tag{5}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-5">(5)</a></span></p>
+<p>We use this as an inherited written analytic theorem, not as a newly kernel-checked result. Composing the new upper certificates with that theorem and the formalizer’s endpoint criterion gives an explicit control certificate for the original family.</p>
+<p>For a packet consisting exactly of an off-line quartet, the already-established endpoint lower bound additionally implies the following new representative-level consequence: among the two indicated degree restrictions, at least one squared singular value is at most</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-6"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mo>exp</mo><mspace width="-0.167em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mi>−</mi><mo>arsinh</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{\exp\!\left[-\operatorname{arsinh}
+                \left(\frac{\delta k}{2C_h}\right)\right].}       \tag{6}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-6">(6)</a></span></p>
+<p>This is a necessary consequence of an off-line quartet, not an assertion that small singular values imply one. The calibration below retains a nilpotent on-line block with nontrivial restriction loss.</p>
+<p>The general matrix and exterior mechanisms are classical. The contribution is their explicit propagation through this programme’s actual source, full-jet quotient, support transitions, and arithmetic action. No upper estimate uniform in <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> for the logarithmic loss is proved in this note.</p>
+</section>
+<section id="preserve-the-original-base-and-source" class="level1">
+<h1>2. Preserve the original base and source</h1>
+<p>Retain the marked coefficient square</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-7"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mover><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mover></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo>↓</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mo>↓</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ℂ</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mi>ℤ</mi></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>j</mi></mover></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>.</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+\begin{array}{ccc}
+G(\mathbb Z)&amp;\xrightarrow{G(j)}&amp;G(\mathbb C)\\
+p_{\mathbb Z}\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow p_{\mathbb C}\\
+\mathbb Z&amp;\xrightarrow{j}&amp;\mathbb C.
+\end{array}                                                       \tag{7}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-7">(7)</a></span></p>
+<p>Here <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">{</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">}</mo><mo>⊔</mo><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">{</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>•</mo></msup><mo>:</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(R)=\{\tau\}\sqcup\{r^\bullet:r\in R\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mn>0</mn><mi>R</mi><mo>•</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_R=0_R^\bullet</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>0</mn><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_R(\tau)=0_R</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>•</mo></msup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_R(r^\bullet)=r</annotation></semantics></math>. The target of <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathbb Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is infinite. The structural base remains <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔟</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak b_\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. Polynomial degree and relation depth do not adjoin another scalar zero.</p>
+<p>Keep</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-8"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mi>V</mi><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>Θ</mi></mover><mi>ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℬ</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>Θ</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>−</mi><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>∂</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>ξ</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ C_+=[V\xrightarrow{\Theta}\mathscr B],\quad
+ Q=\mathscr B/\Theta V,\quad D=-x\partial_x,\quad g=2\xi.          \tag{8}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-8">(8)</a></span></p>
+<p>For a fixed finite packet, retain every selected zero with its complete order:</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-9"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mi>ℳ</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ h(s)=\prod_{\rho\in Z}(s-\rho)^{m_\rho},\qquad
+ v_h=g/h,\qquad\mathcal MF_h=v_h.                                \tag{9}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-9">(9)</a></span></p>
+<p>The arithmetic integration density at tensor degree <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-10"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>*</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ w_h(t)=\frac{|v_h(1/2+it)|^2}{2\pi},\quad
+ m_{h,k}=w_h^{*k},\quad
+ \mu_h=\int w_h,\quad\int m_{h,k}=\mu_h^k.                        \tag{10}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-10">(10)</a></span></p>
+<p>On the existing cyclic sum module use</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-11"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>deg</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ E=\mathbb C[S]/(\chi_{h,k}),\quad q=\deg\chi_{h,k},\quad
+ A=M_S,\quad S=s_1+\cdots+s_k,\quad c=k/2.                        \tag{11}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-11">(11)</a></span></p>
+<p>The canonical arithmetic source is</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-12"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒱</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>⊗</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \mathcal V P=P(D_1+\cdots+D_k)(F_h^{\otimes k}),                 \tag{12}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-12">(12)</a></span></p>
+<p>and its norm is</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-13"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">∥</mo><mi>𝒱</mi><mi>P</mi><msup><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \|\mathcal V P\|^2
+ =\int_{\mathbb R}|P(c+iu)|^2m_{h,k}(u)\,du.                     \tag{13}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-13">(13)</a></span></p>
+<p>The full arithmetic coefficient maps remain</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-14"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></msup><mi>𝒱</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>η</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>χ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></msup><mi>𝒱</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>⊗</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mi>η</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>χ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>υ</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>⊗</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>υ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ J^{(k)}\mathcal V=\eta\pi_\chi,\qquad
+ q^{(k)}\mathcal V=\sigma_h^{\otimes k}\eta\pi_\chi,\qquad
+ \eta[P]=\upsilon_h^{\otimes k}P(A_k)1,\quad
+ \upsilon_h=j_h(g/h).                                            \tag{14}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-14">(14)</a></span></p>
+<p>All derivatives and arithmetic units in these jets remain. The quotient coordinate <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><mi>χ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[P]_\chi</annotation></semantics></math> and its arithmetic image <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><mi>χ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta[P]_\chi</annotation></semantics></math> are connected by this specified injective module map.</p>
+<p>Let <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_0,p_1,\ldots</annotation></semantics></math> be the original monic <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-polynomials orthogonal for (13), with squared norms <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_0=\mu_h^k</annotation></semantics></math>. No polynomial is divided by its norm.</p>
+<p>For <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\ge q-1</annotation></semantics></math>, let</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-15"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>𝒟</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>χ</mi><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \mathcal P_N=\mathbb C[S]_{\le N},\qquad
+ \mathcal D_N=\chi\mathcal P_{N-q},\qquad
+ \mathcal P_{-1}=0.                                               \tag{15}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-15">(15)</a></span></p>
+<p>The exact source sequence is</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-16"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mover><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi>×</mi><mi>χ</mi></mrow></mover><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mover><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>χ</mi></msub></mover><mi>E</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ 0\longrightarrow\mathcal P_{N-q}
+ \xrightarrow{\times\chi}\mathcal P_N
+ \xrightarrow{\pi_\chi}E\longrightarrow0.                         \tag{16}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-16">(16)</a></span></p>
+<p>Monicity proves surjectivity and the displayed kernel, including at <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=q-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Under <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal V</annotation></semantics></math>, the relation <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi P</annotation></semantics></math> is the same original theta boundary whose primitive was constructed by fixed-order division into the tensor factors. Its support label and both occurrences of every Koszul sign remain in that primitive.</p>
+<p>All finite Gram inverses in the note require <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\ge1</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=1</annotation></semantics></math> the finite arithmetic packet is zero, all displayed determinant-line observations have empty determinant one, and no positive-rank inverse is introduced. Its analytic source and mass remain as in (10).</p>
+</section>
+<section id="construct-the-canonical-degree-transport-and-its-adjoint" class="level1">
+<h1>3. Construct the canonical degree transport and its adjoint</h1>
+<p>Use the ordered, unscaled basis <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_0,\ldots,p_N)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal P_N</annotation></semantics></math>. Define</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-17"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>diag</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><mi>χ</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ O_N=\operatorname{diag}(\omega_0,\ldots,\omega_N),\quad
+ B_N=[b_0,\ldots,b_N],\quad b_n=[p_n]_\chi.                         \tag{17}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-17">(17)</a></span></p>
+<p>Here <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>→</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_N:\mathbb C^{N+1}\to E</annotation></semantics></math> is the remainder map in the fixed quotient basis <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,S,\ldots,S^{q-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Put</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-18"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>*</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>*</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ K_N=B_NO_N^{-1}B_N^*,\qquad G_N=K_N^{-1},\qquad
+ R_N=O_N^{-1}B_N^*G_N:E\to\mathcal P_N.                            \tag{18}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-18">(18)</a></span></p>
+<p>The formula gives</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-19"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>*</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mo>im</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒟</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msubsup><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ B_NR_N=I_E,\quad R_N^*O_NR_N=G_N,\quad
+ \operatorname{im}R_N=\mathcal D_N^\perp.                          \tag{19}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-19">(19)</a></span></p>
+<p>Indeed <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_N</annotation></semantics></math> is onto, so <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_N</annotation></semantics></math> is positive definite. For <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo>ker</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\ker B_N</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>*</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^*O_NR_N=z^*B_N^*G_N=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Every representative is <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_Nu+z</annotation></semantics></math>; the two summands are orthogonal. Thus (18) is the unique minimum-norm representative for the original relation space, not an independently selected form.</p>
+<p>The actual test-function representative is <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒱</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal V R_N</annotation></semantics></math>. Every identity involving <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_N</annotation></semantics></math> below passes to that source by (12)–(14).</p>
+<p>For <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\le j</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>↪</mo><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i,j}:\mathcal P_i\hookrightarrow\mathcal P_j</annotation></semantics></math> be coefficient padding and let <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i,j}:\mathcal P_j\to\mathcal P_j</annotation></semantics></math> keep exactly the first <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i+1</annotation></semantics></math> monic coordinates. Since the full monic family is orthogonal, this is the orthogonal projection onto the original lower-degree source. One has</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-20"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>*</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ B_jL_{i,j}=B_i,\qquad L_{i,j}^*O_jL_{i,j}=O_i.                   \tag{20}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-20">(20)</a></span></p>
+<p>Let <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>ℰ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr E_N</annotation></semantics></math> denote <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> with its actual quotient metric <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_N</annotation></semantics></math>. The original quotient transport is</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-21"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>ℰ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mi>u</mi><mo>↦</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ I_{i,j}:\mathscr E_i\to\mathscr E_j,\quad u\mapsto u,
+ \qquad B_jL_{i,j}=I_{i,j}B_i.                                    \tag{21}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-21">(21)</a></span></p>
+<p>It intertwines <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> exactly. Its Gram decreases, because the larger source admits the old representative and additional original relations:</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-22"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≼</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ G_j\preceq G_i.                                                  \tag{22}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-22">(22)</a></span></p>
+<p>Its metric adjoint is the map in (1). To prove it, compute</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-23"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">⟨</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><msub><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">⟩</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">⟨</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mi>v</mi><msub><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">⟩</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \langle I_{i,j}u,v\rangle_{G_j}
+ =u^*G_jv
+ =u^*G_i(K_iG_jv)
+ =\langle u,T_{i,j}v\rangle_{G_i}.                                \tag{23}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-23">(23)</a></span></p>
+<p>This is an adjoint of the <strong>existing</strong> transport. It is not asserted to be a unital algebra homomorphism.</p>
+<p>Direct substitution into (18) proves the representative restriction identity</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-24"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{L_{i,j}R_iT_{i,j}=P_{i,j}R_j.}                             \tag{24}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-24">(24)</a></span></p>
+<p>For example, the first <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i+1</annotation></semantics></math> coordinates of <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_j</annotation></semantics></math> are <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>*</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_i^{-1}B_i^*G_j</annotation></semantics></math>; the right coordinates of <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_iT_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>O</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>*</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_i^{-1}B_i^*G_iK_iG_j</annotation></semantics></math>, which are identical.</p>
+<p>The adjoints compose exactly:</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-25"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ T_{i,j}T_{j,l}=K_iG_jK_jG_l=K_iG_l=T_{i,l}.                      \tag{25}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-25">(25)</a></span></p>
+<p>This agrees with contravariance of metric adjoints applied to the original identity transports. There is no intervening choice of a metric.</p>
+</section>
+<section id="record-the-arithmetic-action-and-the-actual-boundary-correction" class="level1">
+<h1>4. Record the arithmetic action and the actual boundary correction</h1>
+<p>Set</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-26"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖧</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>𝖵</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \mathsf H_N=G_N^{-1}(A^*G_N+G_NA-kG_N),\quad
+ W_N=A^*G_N+G_NA-kG_N,\quad
+ \mathsf V_N=AK_N+K_NA^*-kK_N.                                   \tag{26}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-26">(26)</a></span></p>
+<p>The adjoint-action identity gives</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-27"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝖧</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>𝖧</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝖵</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{AT_{i,j}-T_{i,j}A
+ =\mathsf H_iT_{i,j}-T_{i,j}\mathsf H_j
+ =\mathsf V_iG_j-K_iW_j.}                                       \tag{27}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-27">(27)</a></span></p>
+<p>For a direct proof, expand the rightmost expression and cancel the two occurrences of <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_iA^*G_j</annotation></semantics></math> and of <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">kK_iG_j</annotation></semantics></math>. This records the full equivariance defect of the return map. The forward transport (21) is equivariant. The return need not be equivariant for the arithmetic action.</p>
+<p>If <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_i,\epsilon_j</annotation></semantics></math> are the actual operator norms of <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖧</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝖧</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf H_i,\mathsf H_j</annotation></semantics></math> in their respective metrics, (22)–(23) imply the finite bound</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-28"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">∥</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mi>A</mi><msub><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℰ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \|AT_{i,j}-T_{i,j}A\|_{\mathscr E_j\to\mathscr E_i}
+ \le\epsilon_i+\epsilon_j.                                      \tag{28}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-28">(28)</a></span></p>
+<p>No spectral subspace of the return map is therefore declared invariant under <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> merely because it is a useful metric diagnostic. Equation (27) is the explicit comparison.</p>
+<p>Define the degree-restriction loss endomorphism</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-29"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ H_{i,j}=I_E-T_{i,j}=(K_j-K_i)G_j.                                \tag{29}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-29">(29)</a></span></p>
+<p>The original relation correction is</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-30"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msub><mi>𝔡</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{\mathfrak d_{i,j}=L_{i,j}R_i-R_j
+ =L_{i,j}R_iH_{i,j}-(I-P_{i,j})R_j.}                              \tag{30}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-30">(30)</a></span></p>
+<p>Both terms on the right have arithmetic remainder <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Their difference has zero remainder. Consequently</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-31"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>𝔡</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mo>im</mo><msub><mi>𝔡</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>𝒟</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msubsup><mi>𝔡</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>*</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>𝔡</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ B_j\mathfrak d_{i,j}=0,\quad
+ \operatorname{im}\mathfrak d_{i,j}\subseteq
+ \mathcal D_j\cap\mathcal D_i^\perp,\quad
+ \mathfrak d_{i,j}^*O_j\mathfrak d_{i,j}=G_i-G_j.                  \tag{31}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-31">(31)</a></span></p>
+<p>The Gram identity follows because <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_j</annotation></semantics></math> is orthogonal to every relation in <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒟</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal D_j</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the lower-degree canonical representative is the higher-degree representative plus the explicit original relation (30).</p>
+<p>The split map of a single active fibre is</p>
+<p><span class="math display"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo>:</mo><msubsup><mi>ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo>→</mo><msubsup><mi>ℰ</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>•</mo></msup><mo>↦</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>•</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mi>τ</mi><mo>↦</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ I_{i,j}^{\tau}:\mathscr E_i^\tau\to\mathscr E_j^\tau,
+ \quad u^\bullet\mapsto u^\bullet,\quad\tau\mapsto\tau,
+</annotation></semantics></math></span> <span class="math display" id="eq-32"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo>:</mo><msubsup><mi>ℰ</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo>→</mo><msubsup><mi>ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>•</mo></msup><mo>↦</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>•</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mi>τ</mi><mo>↦</mo><mi>τ</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ T_{i,j}^{\tau}:\mathscr E_j^\tau\to\mathscr E_i^\tau,
+ \quad u^\bullet\mapsto(T_{i,j}u)^\bullet,\quad\tau\mapsto\tau.    \tag{32}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-32">(32)</a></span></p>
+<p>These are <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\mathbb C)</annotation></semantics></math>-linear single-fibre maps. The forward maps embed into the original directed support diagram. The reverse maps are the metric-adjoint diagram; an additional naturality across the original forward transports is not presumed. Their actual reverse-composition identity is (25). Inclusion of a single-fibre lift into the reconstructed carrier sends <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>•</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> to <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mo>ℓ</mo><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ell_N,u)</annotation></semantics></math> and its external <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> to the carrier’s external zero.</p>
+<p>Under the original degree-<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> internal quotient, a supported value of <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔡</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak d_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> becomes <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mo>ℓ</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ell_j,0)</annotation></semantics></math>; external absence remains external absence. The relation before that map remains available in (30)–(31), including its original theta primitive.</p>
+</section>
+<section id="exact-volume-as-a-restriction-spectrum" class="level1">
+<h1>5. Exact volume as a restriction spectrum</h1>
+<p>The endomorphism <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> is positive self-adjoint in both <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_j</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-33"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ G_iT_{i,j}=G_j,\qquad
+ G_jT_{i,j}=G_jK_iG_j.                                           \tag{33}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-33">(33)</a></span></p>
+<p>Also <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≺</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≼</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\prec T_{i,j}\preceq I</annotation></semantics></math> in either metric. For <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_i</annotation></semantics></math>, this is (22); for <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_j</annotation></semantics></math>, use <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≼</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_i\preceq K_j</annotation></semantics></math>. Let its <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> eigenvalues, with multiplicities, be <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_a\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
+<p>The singular values of <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>ℰ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>ℰ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i,j}:\mathscr E_i\to\mathscr E_j</annotation></semantics></math> are exactly <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msqrt><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{g_a}</annotation></semantics></math> because <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i,j}^\dagger I_{i,j}=T_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>. The same nonzero singular values belong to <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> in the reverse Hilbert types and to the source restriction (24). This is the concrete principal-angle construction for the canonical image and the lower-degree source; it uses their actual inner products.</p>
+<p>Taking determinants gives</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-34"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mo>det</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>det</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>det</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mo>log</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>−</mi><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>a</mi></munder><mo>log</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{\det T_{i,j}=\frac{\det K_i}{\det K_j}
+ =\frac{V_j}{V_i},\qquad
+ \mathcal L_{i,j}:=\log\frac{V_i}{V_j}=-\sum_a\log g_a.}          \tag{34}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-34">(34)</a></span></p>
+<p>The top exterior map <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>⋀</mo><mi>q</mi></msup><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigwedge^qT_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> acts by this literal determinant in the fixed determinant coordinates. With the source’s unscaled alternating tensor representative, the determinant vector has squared norm <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mi>!</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q!V_N</annotation></semantics></math> at degree <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. Both factorials remain in the ratio. This is determinant-line accounting through the same source–relation sequence as in the previous Deligne-oriented construction, not an imported purity conclusion.</p>
+<p>An equivalent exact count is</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-35"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msub><mi>ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mfrac><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">}</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></mfrac><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>∞</mi></msubsup><mi>#</mi><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">}</mo><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{\mathcal L_{i,j}
+ =\int_0^1\frac{\#\{a:g_a&lt;t\}}{t}\,dt
+ =\int_0^\infty\#\{a:g_a&lt;e^{-s}\}\,ds.}                         \tag{35}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-35">(35)</a></span></p>
+<p>For one <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, the first integral is <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_g^1dt/t</annotation></semantics></math>; summing proves the formula. The second uses <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=e^{-s}</annotation></semantics></math>. Values at equality do not affect the integrals.</p>
+<p>For the quartet endpoint window, (34) is applied twice, to</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-36"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ (i_0,j_0)=(q-1,2q-1),\qquad(i_1,j_1)=(q,2q).                   \tag{36}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-36">(36)</a></span></p>
+<p>Their losses add to the original four-volume quantity <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal B_{h,k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
+</section>
+<section id="an-exterior-expansion-retains-every-full-jet-basis-weight" class="level1">
+<h1>6. An exterior expansion retains every full-jet basis weight</h1>
+<p>For a <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-element subset <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">{</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>⋯</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">}</mo><mo>⊆</mo><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\{d_1&lt;\cdots&lt;d_q\}\subseteq\{0,\ldots,j\}</annotation></semantics></math> define</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-37"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mo>det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><munder><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ a_D=\frac{|\det[b_{d_1},\ldots,b_{d_q}]|^2}
+                 {\prod_{d\in D}\omega_d}.                     \tag{37}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-37">(37)</a></span></p>
+<p>Cauchy–Binet applied to (18) gives</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-38"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mo>det</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{\det K_j=\sum_{|D|=q}a_D.}                              \tag{38}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-38">(38)</a></span></p>
+<p>This is an identity of positive finite scalar observations, with no assignment of the sum to one. The actual exterior map giving the numerator is</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-39"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mover><mo>⋀</mo><mi>q</mi></mover><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><mover><mo>⋀</mo><mi>q</mi></mover><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><mo>det</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>∧</mo><mi>⋯</mi><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></msub><mo>↦</mo><mo>det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∧</mo><mi>⋯</mi><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \bigwedge^q B_j:\bigwedge^q\mathcal P_j\to\det E,
+ \quad p_{d_1}\wedge\cdots\wedge p_{d_q}
+       \mapsto\det[b_{d_1},\ldots,b_{d_q}]\,e_0\wedge\cdots\wedge e_{q-1}.
+                                                                    \tag{39}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-39">(39)</a></span></p>
+<p>The squared norm of the source wedge is <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod_{d\in D}\omega_d</annotation></semantics></math>. The determinant-coordinate line used to read its coefficient is specified; it is not the canonical arithmetic Gram reset to the identity.</p>
+<p>Introduce the finite scalar polynomial</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-40"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ Z_{i,j}(z)=\det(K_i+z(K_j-K_i)).                                 \tag{40}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-40">(40)</a></span></p>
+<p>Multilinearity and Cauchy–Binet prove the full coefficient identity</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-41"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>#</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>∩</mo><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">}</mo><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{Z_{i,j}(z)=
+ \sum_{|D|=q}a_Dz^{\#(D\cap\{i+1,\ldots,j\})}.}                \tag{41}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-41">(41)</a></span></p>
+<p>In particular <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>det</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(0)=\det K_i&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>det</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(1)=\det K_j</annotation></semantics></math>, and</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-42"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>det</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∏</mo><mi>a</mi></munder><mo minsize="1.2" maxsize="1.2" stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo minsize="1.2" maxsize="1.2" stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mo><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{\frac{Z_{i,j}(z)}{\det K_j}
+ =\det(T_{i,j}+zH_{i,j})
+ =\prod_a\bigl(g_a+z(1-g_a)\bigr).}                              \tag{42}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-42">(42)</a></span></p>
+<p>The scalar division is the displayed observation map from the retained pair <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>det</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z,\det K_j)</annotation></semantics></math>; the original total and mass remain in (38)–(41). This determinant generating mechanism is classical in projection determinantal measures. Its present interpretation concerns the original degree-admission and full-jet maps, not a randomly selected alternative arithmetic source.</p>
+<p>The first two occupation ratios are</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-43"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>Tr</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mfrac><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mi>″</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>Tr</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \frac{Z&#39;(1)}{Z(1)}=\operatorname{Tr}H_{i,j},\qquad
+ \frac{Z&#39;&#39;(1)+Z&#39;(1)}{Z(1)}
+ -\left(\frac{Z&#39;(1)}{Z(1)}\right)^2
+ =\operatorname{Tr}(H_{i,j}-H_{i,j}^2).                           \tag{43}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-43">(43)</a></span></p>
+<p>These identities follow by differentiating (42); every denominator is the original positive scalar total. The weighted counts in (41) remain available before taking those ratios.</p>
+<p>There are two explicit ways a term can be missing from a scalar total. An admitted subset with dependent full-jet columns has <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_D=0</annotation></semantics></math>, a supported zero. A subset containing an index above the admitted degree is outside that finite indexing object, and its extension to a larger indexing object is assigned external absence. For example, in the calibration <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi=(S-1)^2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_2</annotation></semantics></math> are dependent: the subset <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> is admitted and has supported zero weight. The subset <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,4\}</annotation></semantics></math> is absent from degree three. The algebraic specialization <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=0</annotation></semantics></math> in (41) sends each positive-degree monomial to a supported scalar zero; its support record is not retroactively deleted.</p>
+<p>Full derivatives are retained under a change to raw confluent jet coordinates. For roots <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_a</annotation></semantics></math> of orders <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_a</annotation></semantics></math>, let</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-44"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>χ</mi></msub><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>∂</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">{</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left"><mfrac displaystyle="true"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>!</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>!</mi></mrow></mfrac><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left"><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left"><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign="left" style="text-align: left"><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mi>.</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ C_\chi{}_{(a,r),b}
+ =\left.\partial_S^rS^b\right|_{S=\lambda_a}
+ =\begin{cases}\dfrac{b!}{(b-r)!}\lambda_a^{b-r},&amp;b\ge r,\\0,&amp;b&lt;r.
+ \end{cases}                                                     \tag{44}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-44">(44)</a></span></p>
+<p>Its determinant, in the stated block order, is</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-45"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>det</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>χ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><munder><mo>∏</mo><mi>a</mi></munder><munderover><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>r</mi><mi>!</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><munder><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \det C_\chi
+ =\left(\prod_a\prod_{r=0}^{m_a-1}r!\right)
+  \prod_{a&lt;b}(\lambda_b-\lambda_a)^{m_am_b}.                      \tag{45}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-45">(45)</a></span></p>
+<p>For any explicitly retained invertible arithmetic unit matrix <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_u</annotation></semantics></math>, the quotient-coordinate change is <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>χ</mi></msub><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_\chi M_u</annotation></semantics></math>. Every weight (37) acquires the same factor <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">|</mo><mo>det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>χ</mi></msub><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\det(C_\chi M_u)|^2</annotation></semantics></math>, both totals acquire it, and (34), (42) are unchanged through that specified ratio. The action and return are conjugated by this matrix, and the canonical representative is precomposed with its inverse. No nilpotent derivative order is removed.</p>
+<p>For the actual cyclic inclusion <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>↪</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta:E\hookrightarrow B_k^{S_k}</annotation></semantics></math>, the ambient matrix can be rectangular. Its correct exterior comparison is</p>
+<p><span class="math display"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mover><mo>⋀</mo><mi>q</mi></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>η</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mo>det</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mover><mo>⋀</mo><mi>q</mi></mover><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∧</mo><mi>⋯</mi><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \bigwedge^q(\eta B_D)
+ = (\det B_D)(\bigwedge^q\eta)(e_0\wedge\cdots\wedge e_{q-1}).
+</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
+<p>The fixed nonzero image vector is retained. There is no determinant of a rectangular matrix being inverted. A square coordinate change <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>χ</mi></msub><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_\chi M_u</annotation></semantics></math> applies only on a specified coordinate model of the finite image, or for an actual unit endomorphism of <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>. In the ambient symmetric module the original map remains <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, including the complete factor <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msubsup><mi>υ</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>⊗</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\upsilon_h^{\otimes k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
+</section>
+<section id="a-certified-finite-logarithmic-upper-bound" class="level1">
+<h1>7. A certified finite logarithmic upper bound</h1>
+<p>Let <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=H_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>Tr</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_m=\operatorname{Tr}(H^m)</annotation></semantics></math>. Positivity in the original metric implies <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_m\ge0</annotation></semantics></math>, and the eigenvalues lie in <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-46"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msub><mi>ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo accent="false">∞</mo></munderover><mfrac><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{\mathcal L_{i,j}=\sum_{m=1}^{\infty}\frac{s_m}{m}.}       \tag{46}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-46">(46)</a></span></p>
+<p>A valid rational or real lower gap <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_0&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is supplied by the <strong>actual matrix inequality</strong></p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-47"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≽</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{K_i\succeq g_0K_j.}                                      \tag{47}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-47">(47)</a></span></p>
+<p>It gives <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>≼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\preceq(1-g_0)I</annotation></semantics></math> in <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_j</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1-g_0</annotation></semantics></math>. For integer <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\ge1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;r&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, set</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-48"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>−</mi><mo>log</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></munderover><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mi>/</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ c_p(r)=\frac{-\log(1-r)-\sum_{m=1}^pr^m/m}{r^{p+1}}.            \tag{48}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-48">(48)</a></span></p>
+<p>The finite enclosure is</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-49"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></munderover><mfrac><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></munderover><mfrac><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{
+ \sum_{m=1}^p\frac{s_m}{m}
+ \le\mathcal L_{i,j}
+ \le U_p(H,g_0):=
+ \sum_{m=1}^p\frac{s_m}{m}+c_p(r)s_{p+1}.}                        \tag{49}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-49">(49)</a></span></p>
+<p>For <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0</annotation></semantics></math>, (47) and <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≼</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_i\preceq K_j</annotation></semantics></math> give <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=0</annotation></semantics></math> and both bounds are zero.</p>
+<p><strong>Proof.</strong> For <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\le x\le r</annotation></semantics></math>, the tail of <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>−</mi><mo>log</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\log(1-x)</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
+<p><span class="math display"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo accent="false">∞</mo></munderover><mfrac><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo accent="false">∞</mo></munderover><mfrac><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ x^{p+1}\sum_{m=p+1}^\infty\frac{x^{m-p-1}}m
+ \le x^{p+1}\sum_{m=p+1}^\infty\frac{r^{m-p-1}}m
+ =c_p(r)x^{p+1}.
+</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
+<p>Apply this scalar inequality to the positive self-adjoint <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and take its trace. For every fixed admitted pair and fixed certified <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_0</annotation></semantics></math>, the lower and upper bounds converge to the same loss as <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. This is fixed-pair convergence, not a uniform assertion in tensor degree.</p>
+<p>Already <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=1</annotation></semantics></math> gives</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-50"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>−</mi><mo>log</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \mathcal L_{i,j}\le s_1+
+ \frac{-\log g_0-(1-g_0)}{(1-g_0)^2}s_2.                          \tag{50}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-50">(50)</a></span></p>
+<p>It retains a second moment rather than replacing every loss by the maximum one. Higher <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> retain the successive moments of the same operator. No eigenvalue of <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is inferred from an eigenvalue of <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>; their actual commutator is (27).</p>
+<p>For the two windows in (36), verified gaps <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_0^{(0)},g_0^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> give</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-51"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><msub><mi>ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{\mathcal B_{h,k}\le
+ U_p(H^{(0)},g_0^{(0)})+U_p(H^{(1)},g_0^{(1)}).}                   \tag{51}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-51">(51)</a></span></p>
+<p>Combining (5), the formalizer’s endpoint theorem, and monotonicity of <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mo>sinh</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sinh</annotation></semantics></math> gives the finite canonical-family estimate</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-52"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><munder><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mi>q</mi><mo>sinh</mo><mspace width="-0.167em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{
+ \min_{q\le N&lt;2q}\epsilon_{h,k,N}
+ \le C_hq\sinh\!\left(
+ \frac{U_p(H^{(0)},g_0^{(0)})+U_p(H^{(1)},g_0^{(1)})}{2q}
+ \right),\quad q\ge k\ge3.}                                     \tag{52}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-52">(52)</a></span></p>
+<p>This theorem uses the explicit matrix certificate (47). Existence of some positive gap for each finite pair does not imply the uniform gap or moment control that would finish the arithmetic argument. Formula (52) states exactly the quantity a certified finite or uniform analytic estimate must bound.</p>
+<section id="error-aware-certificate-types" class="level2">
+<h2>Error-aware certificate types</h2>
+<p>The checker supplies exact rational lower and upper bounds for <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>log</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log x</annotation></semantics></math> on positive rational inputs. It uses <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>e</mi></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=2^e y</annotation></semantics></math>, <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\le y&lt;2</annotation></semantics></math>, and</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-53"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>log</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mfrac><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ℰ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ℰ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \log y=2\sum_{a=0}^{m-1}\frac{t^{2a+1}}{2a+1}+\mathcal E_m,
+ \quad t=\frac{y-1}{y+1},\quad
+ 0\le\mathcal E_m\le
+ \frac{2t^{2m+1}}{(2m+1)(1-t^2)}.                                 \tag{53}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-53">(53)</a></span></p>
+<p>The same formula encloses <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>log</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log2</annotation></semantics></math>; reciprocation handles <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. Finite rational LDL or principal-minor checks certify (47). No floating-point eigenvalue is accepted as proof of that inequality.</p>
+<p>For actual analytic matrix enclosures, a sufficient input is <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>≼</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_i^-\preceq K_i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≼</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_j\preceq K_j^+</annotation></semantics></math> together with <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>≽</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_i^-\succeq g_0K_j^+</annotation></semantics></math>. Then (47) follows by transitivity. The trace moments in (49) also need certified input enclosures, or exact arithmetic; they are not automatically exact because a gap was enclosed. Independently,</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-54"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mo>log</mo><mo>det</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mo>log</mo><mo>det</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \mathcal L_{i,j}\le\log\det K_j^+-\log\det K_i^-
+                                                                    \tag{54}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-54">(54)</a></span></p>
+<p>when both bounding matrices are positive definite. The implemented test fixtures have exact rational entries. This note supplies no interval certificate for the actual arithmetic integrals or root coordinates.</p>
+</section>
+</section>
+<section id="the-off-line-alternative-forces-a-precise-restriction-phenomenon" class="level1">
+<h1>8. The off-line alternative forces a precise restriction phenomenon</h1>
+<p>For the packet consisting exactly of a quartet with displacement <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, height <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and common multiplicity <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, retain</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-55"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ q_k=[1+k(m-1)](k+1)^2.                                           \tag{55}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-55">(55)</a></span></p>
+<p>The inherited endpoint theorem and its proved norm input give</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-56"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>arsinh</mo><mspace width="-0.167em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \mathcal B_{h,k}\ge
+ 2q_k\operatorname{arsinh}\!\left(\frac{\delta k}{2C_h}\right).
+                                                                    \tag{56}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-56">(56)</a></span></p>
+<p>There are <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2q_k</annotation></semantics></math> positive eigenvalues <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_a</annotation></semantics></math> across the two return maps in (36). Equations (34) and (56) imply that at least one satisfies (6): otherwise every <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>−</mi><mo>log</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\log g_a</annotation></semantics></math> would be smaller than the common lower average in (56), contradicting their sum.</p>
+<p>An eigenvector <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> for that <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_a</annotation></semantics></math> is nonzero in the original arithmetic module. Equation (24) and the actual Gram calculation give</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-57"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">∥</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>v</mi><msubsup><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">∥</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">∥</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>v</mi><msubsup><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">∥</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">∥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>v</mi><msubsup><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">∥</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">∥</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>v</mi><msubsup><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">∥</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \boxed{
+ \frac{\|P_{i,j}R_jv\|_{O_j}^2}{\|R_jv\|_{O_j}^2}
+ =g_a,\qquad
+ \frac{\|(I-P_{i,j})R_jv\|_{O_j}^2}{\|R_jv\|_{O_j}^2}=1-g_a.}      \tag{57}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-57">(57)</a></span></p>
+<p>The old portion has arithmetic coordinate <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_av</annotation></semantics></math>, not <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. The exact original representative of <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> at degree <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is recovered by applying <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i,j}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> in (24). The difference from the full degree-<math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> representative is the actual relation (30). Thus no arithmetic class is called absent, and no numerical near-zero is set equal to <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
+<p>The exact distributional statement is stronger than one selected direction:</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-58"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mn>1</mn></munderover><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>∞</mi></msubsup><mi>#</mi><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">{</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>:</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">}</mo><mspace width="0.167em"></mspace><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \sum_{a=0}^1\int_0^\infty
+   \#\{b:g_b^{(a)}&lt;e^{-s}\}\,ds
+ =\mathcal B_{h,k}.                                               \tag{58}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-58">(58)</a></span></p>
+<p>The growing-degree task can now be stated as control of this cumulative small-restriction spectrum, or as the positive trace certificates (51). A proved upper limit below <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> for <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>log</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal B_{h,k}/(q_k\log k)</annotation></semantics></math> would contradict (56). Such a uniform upper bound is not an output of the present finite identities.</p>
+</section>
+<section id="exact-calibration-retaining-nilpotence-and-mass" class="level1">
+<h1>9. Exact calibration retaining nilpotence and mass</h1>
+<p>Use the explicitly declared algebraic fixture</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-59"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ E=\mathbb C[S]/((S-1)^2),\qquad S=1+ix,
+                                                                    \tag{59}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-59">(59)</a></span></p>
+<p>with the Gaussian source <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mi>/</mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7e^{-x^2/2}dx/\sqrt{2\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>, of literal mass seven and variance one. This fixture is not asserted to be a zeta packet. Its source monic polynomials and norms are</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-60"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>n</mi><mi>!</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ p_0=1,\quad p_1=S-1,\quad
+ p_{n+1}=(S-1)p_n+n p_{n-1},\quad \omega_n=7n!.                    \tag{60}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-60">(60)</a></span></p>
+<p>The coordinate basis <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,S-1)</annotation></semantics></math> is connected to <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,S)</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-61"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd columnalign="center" style="text-align: center"><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ C=\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;-1\\0&amp;1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
+ (1,S)C=(1,S-1).                                                  \tag{61}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-61">(61)</a></span></p>
+<p>In the <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,S-1)</annotation></semantics></math> coordinates, the kernel matrices are</p>
+<p><span class="math display"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>diag</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>diag</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>14</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ K_1=\operatorname{diag}(1/7,1/7),\quad
+ K_2=\operatorname{diag}(3/14,1/7),
+</annotation></semantics></math></span> <span class="math display" id="eq-62"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>diag</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>14</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>14</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.0em"></mspace><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>diag</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>15</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>56</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>14</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ K_3=\operatorname{diag}(3/14,5/14),\quad
+ K_4=\operatorname{diag}(15/56,5/14).                              \tag{62}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-62">(62)</a></span></p>
+<p>The action remains <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=I+N</annotation></semantics></math> with <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\ne0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^2=0</annotation></semantics></math>. It is not replaced by its reduced one-dimensional quotient.</p>
+<p>The two return spectra are exactly</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-63"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>Spec</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">}</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mo>Spec</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">{</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">}</mo><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \operatorname{Spec}T_{1,3}=\{2/3,2/5\},\qquad
+ \operatorname{Spec}T_{2,4}=\{4/5,2/5\}.                           \tag{63}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-63">(63)</a></span></p>
+<p>Consequently</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-64"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℬ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>log</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>15</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo>log</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>25</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>log</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><mn>375</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>32</mn><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>2.46119012317068427</mn><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ \mathcal B=\log(15/4)+\log(25/8)=\log(375/32)
+ \approx2.46119012317068427.                                      \tag{64}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-64">(64)</a></span></p>
+<p>For <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1,3}</annotation></semantics></math> the first two trace moments are <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>14</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14/15</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>106</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>225</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">106/225</annotation></semantics></math>; for <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2,4}</annotation></semantics></math> they are <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4/5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2/5</annotation></semantics></math>. Using <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_0=2/5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=2</annotation></semantics></math> in (49) gives the two rigorously enclosed upper bounds whose displayed decimals are</p>
+<p><span class="math display" id="eq-65"><math display="block" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.32854908781965490</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width="2.0em"></mspace><mn>1.14133853675838303</mn><mi>.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">
+ 1.32854908781965490,\qquad1.14133853675838303.                    \tag{65}
+</annotation></semantics></math><a class="eq-number" href="#eq-65">(65)</a></span></p>
+<p>Their sum is below <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mn>2.469887624578038</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.469887624578038</annotation></semantics></math>. The earlier direct bound by <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>Tr</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Tr}(K_i^{-1}(K_j-K_i))</annotation></semantics></math> gives <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>3.75</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2+7/4=3.75</annotation></semantics></math> on these two windows. Thus the new finite certificate makes a strict, verified improvement on the same source and same metrics. The exact logarithmic enclosures, rather than the printed rounded values, certify the comparison.</p>
+<p>The dependent subset <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> in (37) has a supported zero minor because <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">[</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p_2]=[p_0]</annotation></semantics></math> in this quotient. Nevertheless the first derivative coordinate at <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=1</annotation></semantics></math> is retained and makes the quotient dimension two. A distinct-root replacement would not give the same matrices.</p>
+<p>The test suite also contains off-line algebraic fixtures, nonconstant invertible jet units, and repeated confluent derivatives. It does not infer a zeta-zero statement from these fixtures.</p>
+</section>
+<section id="integration-with-the-deligne-oriented-programme" class="level1">
+<h1>10. Integration with the Deligne-oriented programme</h1>
+<p>The same cohomological transition now carries an explicit positive adjoint and a determinant-line norm. Its action covariance is (27), its theta-boundary correction is (30), and its original support quotient is retained in (32). This is a metric calculation on the existing characteristic-zero realization over <math display="inline" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔟</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak b_\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
+<p>Deligne’s use of determinant weights and the compact-to-ordinary image requires an actual estimate on the same image. This note supplies additional calculation and finite upper certificates for that estimate; it does not invoke his finite-field purity hypotheses for the theta object. The inherited source review’s strict inequality in Lemma 3.2.10 and its squared-eigenvalue step in 3.2.13 are retained. This turn did not newly reassemble the Deligne archive or claim a complete rereading.</p>
+<p>Current repository input was checked at main <code>7ea0a49945390eae14d3160a5730858899768b5f</code>. The current endpoint criterion retains the corrected top-exterior case and the actual second-exterior multiplicity cost. No second cost-free exterior amplification is used here. The proof status of the original norm bound (5) remains the inherited written analytic status; the present finite tests do not promote it to a Lean certificate.</p>
+<p>The principal next quantity is no longer only an unnamed ratio of large determinants. It is the explicit log-loss of the original identity transports, with the canonical representative restriction, all original relations, full jets, arithmetic action, and complementary polynomial degrees attached. The uniform bound still needed is precisely the one on that constructed family.</p>
+</section>
